module bindings.python.restricted_eval_nested_scope_name_filter_gap;

// A Python sandbox around eval() decides which names the evaluated
// expression may use. The route CPython documents for that decision is to
// compile the expression and filter the resulting code object's `co_names`
// against an allowlist, and that route is incomplete on its own:
// `co_names` holds only the names the top-level code object itself loads,
// while a lambda body and a generator expression each compile to a
// separate code object that the outer one reaches through `co_consts`.
// Names used only inside the nested scope never reach the filter and still
// execute, so the allowlist is a boundary the expression can step over --
// `len.__self__` written inside a lambda is the canonical recovery of the
// real builtins module from inside a `{'__builtins__': {}}` eval.
//
// The binding reads the guard's own attribute reads rather than the
// spelling of any one fix. It anchors on the builtin `compile()` spelled
// without a receiver (an exact call-path comparison, so `re.compile` and
// every other dotted compile is out of scope) and requires a `co_names`
// read in the same function. The negation is on the `co_consts` attribute
// itself, so a guard that descends into nested code objects satisfies it
// whether the descent is written inline, before or after the `co_names`
// loop, or in a recursive helper called from the same function. Attribute
// names are matched on their own segment with `endsWith` rather than a
// substring, so the receiver's name is irrelevant -- `code.co_names`,
// `c.co_names` and `fn.__code__.co_names` are the same read -- while
// `co_varnames` and `co_filename` do not match.
//
// An expression filter built on the `ast` module never reads `co_names` at
// all, so it is outside this binding either way, as is a filter built on
// `symtable`. Every clause reads AST-derived call-path and attribute
// tokens, so the module's comments cannot satisfy the anchor or silence
// the negation: prose naming `co_consts` is not a descent.
//
// Residual, in the false-positive direction and stated rather than hidden:
// a guard that keeps the `co_names` loop in the filtering function and
// puts the `co_consts` descent in a helper it calls is reported, because
// the enclosing function's context facts do not carry what a callee reads.
// That is the function-local control-attribution limit, not a property of
// this guard. The measured reachable set does not contain the shape: across
// the 94k Python files of the corpus checkouts, six functions read
// `.co_names` and call bare `compile()`, and the one that also reads
// `co_consts` is excluded by the negation.
binding restrictedEvalNameFilterOmitsNestedScopes {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "python"
    and node.context.callPath == "compile"
    and node.context.selector endsWith "co_names"
    and not (node.context.selector endsWith "co_consts")
  emit issue code.RestrictedEvalNestedScopeNameFilterGap at node
}
